# Accepted market-data outage days.
#
# Two line forms, both reviewed findings that have been consciously accepted:
#
#   2026-09-09                  an outage day whose data is unrecoverable
#   coverage ticketnetwork 16   a low coverage mode a flapping rolling source returns to
#
# Everything after `#` is a comment and is the place to record WHY.
#
# The check still reports these - it just stops treating them as fatal. An accepted hole
# stays visible rather than disappearing from the output, for the same reason
# .privacy-accepted prints accepted findings: ops#8 must never fit a sell-timing curve
# across a gap it cannot see.
#
# Committed on purpose, unlike .private-patterns. The run that needs this file is the
# collector's own --strict run on the GitHub runner, which has no local state.
#
# Accept a day here only when the data is genuinely unrecoverable AND the cause is
# understood. A gap whose cause is unknown is a broken collector, and belongs red.

# 2026-09-09, 2026-09-10 - the agent loop stopped when a usage limit blocked a tick
# (ops#135), and at that time the collector workflow ran `check_data_freshness.py
# --strict` BEFORE committing aggregates, so a red gate discarded the day's observations
# instead of keeping them. Both days are therefore absent from all four sources.
#
# Unrecoverable: no source here has a historical price endpoint, so these two days are
# gone rather than pending.
#
# Fixed forward rather than papered over - the ordering bug that caused the loss is gone
# (PR #6, "persist market observations before gap diagnostics"): aggregates now commit
# before the diagnostic gate, so a future red run keeps its data. These two days are the
# last that can be lost this way.
#
# Accepted 2026-09-13 by Wesley: "i guess outages are somewhat expected from time to time".
2026-09-09
2026-09-10

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Accepted coverage floor: TicketNetwork, 16 games.
#
# WHAT IS ACCEPTED. This source alternates between serving 29 of 44 home games and 16,
# on the same GitHub runner, for the same URL. The full series:
#
#   09-05: 29 (residential)  09-06: 16  09-07: 16  09-08: 29
#   09-11: 29  09-12: 29  09-13: 16  09-14: 16
#
# The first low run is two days and CLOSED - 29 on both sides of it. The second is
# 09-13..09-14, which is still OPEN at the time of writing, so it is at least two days and
# its true length is unknown. Run length has therefore been measured ONCE at 2 and once at
# >= 2; the level has been measured on all eight observation days. That asymmetry is the
# whole argument below, so it is stated rather than rounded to "twice, both times 2".
#
# What it establishes is enough: COVERAGE_DROP_PERSIST_DAYS was set to 2 on 2026-09-13 as
# "the smallest window that distinguishes a flap from a step", against a series that
# ALREADY contained the closed two-day 09-06..09-07 run. The threshold was refuted by the
# data in hand when it was written, and it went red on 2026-09-14 on the flap it was built
# to tolerate.
#
# WHY A LEVEL RATHER THAN A LONGER DURATION. Raising the constant to 3 is the same
# mistake with a bigger number - eight observation days cannot support a run-length
# threshold, and a four-day flap would go red again.
#
# WHY IT IS ACCEPTABLE AT ALL. TickPick and Gametime both cover 44/44, so TicketNetwork is
# supplementary - CLAUDE.md already records the coverage itself as accepted, not fixed. A
# residential proxy is not worth the 13 games.
#
# WHAT A FLOOR ACTUALLY EXEMPTS, stated precisely because the first version of this entry
# overstated it and a reviewer had to measure the difference.
#
# A floor marks days whose coverage BOTH lands at or above it AND falls far enough from
# this source's best day to clear the ordinary drop gate. On those days:
#
#   - a sustained coverage drop is a warning rather than fatal, however long it holds
#   - absent games do not accumulate toward a per-game hole. THIS HALF IS LOAD-BEARING.
#     Without it the floor lasted exactly one observation day: the coverage gate fires
#     only on day two of a low run - its baseline is a sliding three-day lookback - and
#     from day three the thirteen absent games tripped GAME_GAP_DAYS instead. Measured
#     against this store before the fix: a 2-day low run gave 0 fatal findings and a
#     3-day run gave 13, so a longer flap went red HARDER than a short one.
#
# It exempts any sustained level at or above 16, not only 16 itself. That is wider than
# "this source's observed low mode" and is deliberate - the flap level decays with the
# calendar as games are played, so a rule pinned to 16 exactly would stop matching within
# a week. The cost is that a novel intermediate level, say a sustained 20, is exempted
# too.
#
# WHAT IS STILL FATAL. Coverage below 16, and it is worth being exact about which check
# catches it, because the coverage gate alone would not. A sustained 15 does NOT trip the
# coverage gate from a base of 16 - that needs both three games lost and more than a
# quarter, so 16 -> 12 was never fatal and is not fatal now. What catches it is the
# per-game-hole check, which is live again below the floor. Measured on this store with
# three sustained days: 15 -> 14 fatal findings, 12 -> 17, 11 -> 18, 5 -> 24.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH. Why the source flaps. One run cannot separate a cache
# behind TicketNetwork's CDN from a genuine window change, and nothing here is evidence
# either way. If 16 ever becomes permanent this file will record it as accepted rather
# than flag it, which is the honest cost of accepting the level: the flap and a permanent
# step to 16 are indistinguishable to this check by construction.
#
# CONFIDENCE: measured, on 8 observation days for the level and 2 runs for the duration.
# The level is what this entry rests on; the duration is explicitly NOT relied upon.
#
# THE ALTERNATIVE NOT TAKEN, so the next session does not have to rediscover it. A
# high-water-mark rule - "has this source returned to 29 within the last N days?" - would
# distinguish a flap from a permanent step, which is the one capability accepting the
# level gives up. It was rejected because N is a duration threshold wearing different
# clothes, and the duration is the quantity this series cannot support. Revisit it once
# there are enough closed runs to fit N against; that needs weeks, not days.
coverage ticketnetwork 16
